---
import DocLayout from '../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import { siteName } from '../config/site';
---

<DocLayout
  title={`Verify the Privacy Claim - Nothing Leaves Your Device | ${siteName}`}
  description={`Don't trust "nothing leaves your device". Check it: the live security policy this page runs under, a live count of outside requests, and how to confirm it in DevTools.`}
  heading="Check it yourself"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    "Nothing leaves your device" is easy to write and hard to believe. So here is how to check it
    instead: what your browser is enforcing right now, and how to watch the network while a tool
    runs.
  </Fragment>

  <section>
    <h2>What your browser enforces</h2>
    <p>
      Every page ships a Content Security Policy. It tells your browser which addresses this page's
      code may talk to. Ours allows one: this site. The full policies are below, read from the page
      you are on, not copied from a doc, so they can't drift. They look long, so here is how to read
      them.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>connect-src 'self'</code> is the line that matters. It blocks the usual ways a script sends
        data out (fetch, XHR, WebSocket, beacons) to any server but this one. It guards against injected
        code and mistakes. For the code we wrote ourselves, the assurance is that it is open source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>default-src 'self'</code>: anything not listed separately also comes from this site
        only.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>blob:</code> and <code>data:</code> are not web addresses. They are files your own browser
        holds in memory, like the preview of the image you just opened.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>'wasm-unsafe-eval'</code> lets WebAssembly run, which the heavier tools use. It does not
        open any connection.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>object-src 'none'</code> shuts off plugins. <code>base-uri</code> stops an injected tag
        from rewriting where relative addresses point. <code>form-action</code> stops forms from submitting
        anywhere else. Ordinary links aren't covered by any of these.
      </li>
      <li>
        The long <code>sha256-…</code> strings are the scary-looking part, and the harmless one. Each
        is a fingerprint of one small script or style written into the page. Your browser runs an inline
        script only if its fingerprint matches exactly. They are not addresses and can't point anywhere,
        and a script that was injected or changed has a different fingerprint, so it gets blocked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'</code> allows <code>style="…"</code> attributes, which the
        framework needs. Styles can't make network requests here, because images and fonts are limited
        to this site too.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <details>
      <summary>Show the full policies</summary>
      <h3>Embedded in the page</h3>
      <pre id="csp-meta" class="live">Needs JavaScript to read the policy from this page.</pre>
      <h3>Sent as a response header</h3>
      <pre id="csp-header" class="live">Needs JavaScript to read the policy from this page.</pre>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Requests to other sites</h2>
    <p class="live-count">
      Outside requests since this page loaded: <strong id="outside-count">checking</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Read from your browser's performance log by this page's own script, so it only covers this
      page. To check a tool, use the Network tab below.
    </p>
    <ul id="outside-list"></ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Scripts on this page</h2>
    <p>
      Every script this page loaded, from the page itself. Open one to read it. A script from
      another site or a browser extension is listed above but can't be read from here.
    </p>
    <div id="script-list"></div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Watch the network yourself</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Open any tool, then DevTools (F12) and the Network tab.</li>
      <li>Tick "Preserve log" and clear it.</li>
      <li>Drop in a file and run the tool.</li>
      <li>
        Some requests to this site may appear, because a tool loads its own code the first time you
        use it. None goes to another site, and none carries your file.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Or just go offline</h2>
    <p>
      Open a tool and run it once with your connection on, so it has fetched its own code. Then turn
      off your Wi-Fi and run it again on the same page, without reloading. It still works, because
      the work happens on your machine. A tool that needs a server stops the moment you disconnect.
      A fresh page load does need the connection, since this site can't be opened offline.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What does reach a server</h2>
    <p>
      The page and its scripts come from our host, Cloudflare. We keep its request logs and traces
      switched on to watch speed and catch errors. They record the address requested, status and
      timing, and can include standard request details such as your IP address and browser type. We
      don't use them for analytics or ads. Your files are not in them, because they are never sent.
      Details are in <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      One tool, the background remover, downloads a small model once. It comes from this site, not a
      third party, and runs on your device.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why there's no cloud feature</h2>
    <p>
      A feature that has to send your document somewhere breaks the promise, so it doesn't ship
      here. That rules out chat-with-your-PDF and server-side conversion. It is a choice, and the
      reason the claim stays true.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What this doesn't prove</h2>
    <p>
      It covers this site's own code. It can't see a browser extension that reads pages, or a
      machine that is already compromised. And you are trusting the code that was served to you
      today. That is why the project is <a href="/source/">open source</a>: read it, or build it
      yourself.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocLayout>

<script>
  const format = (policy: string) =>
    policy
      .split(';')
      .map((part: string) => part.trim())
      .filter(Boolean)
      .join('\n');

  const meta = document.querySelector('meta[http-equiv="content-security-policy"]');
  const metaBox = document.getElementById('csp-meta');
  if (metaBox) {
    metaBox.textContent = meta
      ? format(meta.getAttribute('content') ?? '')
      : 'No policy embedded in this page.';
  }

  const headerBox = document.getElementById('csp-header');
  if (headerBox) {
    fetch(location.href, { method: 'HEAD' })
      .then((res) => {
        const policy = res.headers.get('content-security-policy');
        headerBox.textContent = policy
          ? format(policy)
          : 'This server sent no policy header (expected on a local dev server).';
      })
      .catch(() => {
        headerBox.textContent = 'Could not read the response headers.';
      });
  }

  const count = document.getElementById('outside-count');
  const outsideList = document.getElementById('outside-list');
  const update = () => {
    if (!count || !outsideList) return;
    const outside = performance
      .getEntriesByType('resource')
      .filter((entry) => new URL(entry.name).origin !== location.origin);
    count.textContent = String(outside.length);
    outsideList.replaceChildren(
      ...outside.map((entry) => {
        const item = document.createElement('li');
        const url = document.createElement('code');
        url.textContent = entry.name;
        const kind = (entry as PerformanceResourceTiming).initiatorType || 'unknown';
        item.append(url, ` (${kind})`);
        return item;
      }),
    );
  };
  update();
  new PerformanceObserver(update).observe({ type: 'resource', buffered: true });

  const scriptList = document.getElementById('script-list');
  if (scriptList) {
    scriptList.replaceChildren(
      ...Array.from(document.scripts).map((script, index) => {
        const details = document.createElement('details');
        const summary = document.createElement('summary');
        const body = document.createElement('pre');
        summary.textContent = script.src
          ? script.src.replace(location.origin, '')
          : `Inline script ${index + 1}`;
        details.append(summary, body);
        details.addEventListener('toggle', async () => {
          if (!details.open || body.textContent) return;
          if (!script.src) {
            body.textContent = script.textContent;
            return;
          }
          body.textContent = 'Loading...';
          try {
            const res = await fetch(script.src);
            body.textContent = await res.text();
          } catch {
            body.textContent = 'Could not read this script.';
          }
        });
        return details;
      }),
    );
  }
</script>

<style>
  .live,
  #script-list :global(pre) {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 0.8rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: rgba(40, 59, 73, 0.05);
    font-size: var(--size-sm);
    line-height: 1.5;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  summary,
  #script-list :global(summary) {
    cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--color-oxblood);
  }

  .live-count {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  code {
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 0.3rem;
    font-size: var(--size-sm);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
